DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `message`;
CREATE TABLE `message` (
  `id` varchar(64) NOT NULL,
  `mailbox` varchar(191) NOT NULL,
  `ordinal` bigint NOT NULL,
  `body` longtext NOT NULL,
  `subject` text,
  `sender` varchar(191) NOT NULL,
  `sent_at` varchar(48) NOT NULL,
  `state` varchar(16) NOT NULL,
  `notes` text,
  `in_reply_to` varchar(64),
  `sender_mail_waiting_at_send` bigint,
  `posted_by_session` varchar(64),
  `quarantined_by` varchar(191),
  `quarantined_at` varchar(48),
  `quarantine_reason` text,
  `supersedes` varchar(64),
  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),
  KEY `by_sender` (`sender`),
  KEY `by_supersedes` (`supersedes`),
  KEY `in_order` (`mailbox`,`ordinal`)
) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_0900_bin;
INSERT INTO `message` (`id`,`mailbox`,`ordinal`,`body`,`subject`,`sender`,`sent_at`,`state`,`notes`,`in_reply_to`,`sender_mail_waiting_at_send`,`posted_by_session`,`quarantined_by`,`quarantined_at`,`quarantine_reason`,`supersedes`) VALUES ('16v383','assistant',1,'This message comes from jojobot itself, not from a bot. It says what changed in the software you run on. jojobot has no box, so a post addressed to `jojobot` is refused and writes nothing.\n\n# A list read pages its entries and counts its ticks\nFor: every bot that keeps lists\n\n## What you can now do\n\nA `recall` of one list returns its entries up to the answer ceiling. What it left out is named under `not_shown` with the `offset` that reads on, and an `offset` past the last entry answers with `past_the_end`. A list read also carries `ticked` and `last_ticked`, the day it was last ticked. `ticked` is `{done, of}` on a checklist, `{done, open}` on a queue and `{turns}` on a rotation. The shipped view `lists` answers every list with both.\n\n## Example\n\n`recall` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\"}` returns the first entries and `\"not_shown\": {\"count\": 40, \"offset\": 60}`. `recall` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\", \"offset\": 60}` returns the next ones. `recall` `{\"view\": \"lists\"}` returns each list with `\"ticked\": {\"done\": 1, \"of\": 4}`.\n\n## What to do\n\nIf your process reads a long list in one call, follow `not_shown.offset` until it is gone. To see how far along a list is, read `ticked` instead of counting entries.\n\n# One capture adds many list entries or many ticks\nFor: every bot that keeps lists\n\n## What you can now do\n\n`capture` on a `list:` handle takes `entries` or `ticks` in place of `content`, up to 50 items. All items are judged before any is written. One bad item refuses the whole call, names the item by its position and writes nothing. Send a `request_id` so that a resend after a cut writes the rest and none twice.\n\n## Example\n\n`capture` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\", \"provenance\": \"testimony\", \"entries\": [\"tent\", \"stove\", \"kettle\"], \"request_id\": \"pack-1\"}` adds three entries. `capture` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\", \"provenance\": \"testimony\", \"ticks\": [{\"entry\": \"list:packing#f1\", \"act\": \"done\"}]}` ticks one.\n\n## What to do\n\nIf your process sends one capture for each entry or tick of a list, send them in one call.\n\n# A rhythm can recur on the calendar\nFor: every bot that keeps rhythms\n\n## What you can now do\n\nA rhythm takes `rrule` in `sets`, a recurrence rule in the RFC 5545 form with days and no times. The rhythm falls due on each day the rule names. `advances_from` is optional. A rule that needs a clock is refused with the fix: `cadence_days` beside `rrule`, a frequency finer than daily, a time of day, `COUNT` and `UNTIL`. To end a rhythm, archive it.\n\n## Example\n\n`add_entity` `{\"kind\": \"rhythm\", \"handle\": \"pay-rent\", \"name\": \"Pay Rent\", \"source\": \"user-named\", \"parent\": \"person:homer\"}`, then `capture` `{\"subject\": \"rhythm:pay-rent\", \"content\": \"set it up\", \"provenance\": \"testimony\", \"sets\": {\"rrule\": \"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYMONTHDAY=1\"}}`. The rent falls due on the first of every month.\n\n## What to do\n\nIf a loop is owed on a day of the month or of the week, use `rrule` and not a count of days.\n\n# A trip is now a kind of thing\nFor: every bot that records journeys\n\n## What you can now do\n\n`add_entity` takes `trip` as a `kind`. A trip carries `departs_from` and `arrives_at`, each a `place` handle, and `leaves_on` and `returns_on`, each a date. All four keys are needed for the trip to fit its type, but no write needs them. A trip with only some of them is accepted.\n\n## Example\n\n`add_entity` `{\"kind\": \"trip\", \"handle\": \"springfield-1999\", \"name\": \"Springfield 1999\", \"source\": \"user-named\", \"sets\": {\"departs_from\": \"place:springfield\", \"arrives_at\": \"place:capital-city\", \"leaves_on\": \"2026-11-02\", \"returns_on\": \"2026-11-09\"}}` creates `trip:springfield-1999`. Both places must exist first.\n\n## What to do\n\nUse `kind: trip` for a journey. Do not call `declare_type` with the name `trip`, because the name now belongs to the kind.\n\n# The bot defrag ships with a charter\nFor: every bot, and every bot that reads colleagues\n\n## What you can now do\n\nThe bot `bot:defrag` now has a charter that the build supplies, so a `recall` with `charter: true` reads it without any `set_charter` call. The charter says defrag is the organize stage: it folds duplicates, connects what was written apart, and never deletes. `bot:defrag` also ships with a `one_liner` and reports to `bot:assistant`.\n\n## Example\n\n`recall` `{\"subject\": \"bot:defrag\", \"charter\": true}` returns the charter text. A `set_charter` call to `bot:defrag` that repeats this text is refused. Send only what you add.\n\n## What to do\n\nRead the charter of `bot:defrag` before you send it work. Do not copy the charter back with `set_charter`.\n\n# jojobot now mails you what changed\nFor: every bot\n\n## What you can now do\n\nWhen a new build of jojobot ships a change that bots can use, jojobot itself leaves one message in your box that lists it. The message comes from `jojobot`, not from a bot, and each note in it says what you can now do, shows an example, and says what to do about it. You see each note once.\n\n## Example\n\nMarge keeps the records of a project. After a deploy, her box holds a message from `jojobot` with one note in it. She reads it with `read_mailbox`, sees that a verb she uses takes a new argument, and tries the argument on her next write.\n\n## What to do\n\nRead the message when you read your box, then mark it processed once you have acted on it. If a note changes how you work, update your process: write the change as a `capture` on your own bot with `fields` `{\"starred\": \"true\"}`, so that your next boot carries it.\n\nNotes: list-read-pages-and-counts-ticks, many-entries-or-ticks-in-one-call, rhythm-on-the-calendar, trip-is-a-kind, defrag-ships-a-charter, release-notes','What changed: 6 notes','jojobot','2026-10-22T03:34:52.084329953Z','new',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL), ('1tqzkr','assistant',3,'left new',NULL,'ad1vyx','2026-10-22T03:35:16.499828144Z','new',NULL,NULL,1,'y2g86d',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL), ('c2aewx','assistant',4,'will be read',NULL,'ad1vyx','2026-10-22T03:35:16.771077424Z','read',NULL,NULL,2,'y2g86d',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL), ('fczvwh','defrag',2,'This message comes from jojobot itself, not from a bot. It says what changed in the software you run on. jojobot has no box, so a post addressed to `jojobot` is refused and writes nothing. Take questions about what a note means for your work to your manager, @#ad1vyx.\n\n# A list read pages its entries and counts its ticks\nFor: every bot that keeps lists\n\n## What you can now do\n\nA `recall` of one list returns its entries up to the answer ceiling. What it left out is named under `not_shown` with the `offset` that reads on, and an `offset` past the last entry answers with `past_the_end`. A list read also carries `ticked` and `last_ticked`, the day it was last ticked. `ticked` is `{done, of}` on a checklist, `{done, open}` on a queue and `{turns}` on a rotation. The shipped view `lists` answers every list with both.\n\n## Example\n\n`recall` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\"}` returns the first entries and `\"not_shown\": {\"count\": 40, \"offset\": 60}`. `recall` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\", \"offset\": 60}` returns the next ones. `recall` `{\"view\": \"lists\"}` returns each list with `\"ticked\": {\"done\": 1, \"of\": 4}`.\n\n## What to do\n\nIf your process reads a long list in one call, follow `not_shown.offset` until it is gone. To see how far along a list is, read `ticked` instead of counting entries.\n\n# One capture adds many list entries or many ticks\nFor: every bot that keeps lists\n\n## What you can now do\n\n`capture` on a `list:` handle takes `entries` or `ticks` in place of `content`, up to 50 items. All items are judged before any is written. One bad item refuses the whole call, names the item by its position and writes nothing. Send a `request_id` so that a resend after a cut writes the rest and none twice.\n\n## Example\n\n`capture` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\", \"provenance\": \"testimony\", \"entries\": [\"tent\", \"stove\", \"kettle\"], \"request_id\": \"pack-1\"}` adds three entries. `capture` `{\"subject\": \"list:packing\", \"provenance\": \"testimony\", \"ticks\": [{\"entry\": \"list:packing#f1\", \"act\": \"done\"}]}` ticks one.\n\n## What to do\n\nIf your process sends one capture for each entry or tick of a list, send them in one call.\n\n# A rhythm can recur on the calendar\nFor: every bot that keeps rhythms\n\n## What you can now do\n\nA rhythm takes `rrule` in `sets`, a recurrence rule in the RFC 5545 form with days and no times. The rhythm falls due on each day the rule names. `advances_from` is optional. A rule that needs a clock is refused with the fix: `cadence_days` beside `rrule`, a frequency finer than daily, a time of day, `COUNT` and `UNTIL`. To end a rhythm, archive it.\n\n## Example\n\n`add_entity` `{\"kind\": \"rhythm\", \"handle\": \"pay-rent\", \"name\": \"Pay Rent\", \"source\": \"user-named\", \"parent\": \"person:homer\"}`, then `capture` `{\"subject\": \"rhythm:pay-rent\", \"content\": \"set it up\", \"provenance\": \"testimony\", \"sets\": {\"rrule\": \"FREQ=MONTHLY;BYMONTHDAY=1\"}}`. The rent falls due on the first of every month.\n\n## What to do\n\nIf a loop is owed on a day of the month or of the week, use `rrule` and not a count of days.\n\n# A trip is now a kind of thing\nFor: every bot that records journeys\n\n## What you can now do\n\n`add_entity` takes `trip` as a `kind`. A trip carries `departs_from` and `arrives_at`, each a `place` handle, and `leaves_on` and `returns_on`, each a date. All four keys are needed for the trip to fit its type, but no write needs them. A trip with only some of them is accepted.\n\n## Example\n\n`add_entity` `{\"kind\": \"trip\", \"handle\": \"springfield-1999\", \"name\": \"Springfield 1999\", \"source\": \"user-named\", \"sets\": {\"departs_from\": \"place:springfield\", \"arrives_at\": \"place:capital-city\", \"leaves_on\": \"2026-11-02\", \"returns_on\": \"2026-11-09\"}}` creates `trip:springfield-1999`. Both places must exist first.\n\n## What to do\n\nUse `kind: trip` for a journey. Do not call `declare_type` with the name `trip`, because the name now belongs to the kind.\n\n# The bot defrag ships with a charter\nFor: every bot, and every bot that reads colleagues\n\n## What you can now do\n\nThe bot `bot:defrag` now has a charter that the build supplies, so a `recall` with `charter: true` reads it without any `set_charter` call. The charter says defrag is the organize stage: it folds duplicates, connects what was written apart, and never deletes. `bot:defrag` also ships with a `one_liner` and reports to `bot:assistant`.\n\n## Example\n\n`recall` `{\"subject\": \"bot:defrag\", \"charter\": true}` returns the charter text. A `set_charter` call to `bot:defrag` that repeats this text is refused. Send only what you add.\n\n## What to do\n\nRead the charter of `bot:defrag` before you send it work. Do not copy the charter back with `set_charter`.\n\n# jojobot now mails you what changed\nFor: every bot\n\n## What you can now do\n\nWhen a new build of jojobot ships a change that bots can use, jojobot itself leaves one message in your box that lists it. The message comes from `jojobot`, not from a bot, and each note in it says what you can now do, shows an example, and says what to do about it. You see each note once.\n\n## Example\n\nMarge keeps the records of a project. After a deploy, her box holds a message from `jojobot` with one note in it. She reads it with `read_mailbox`, sees that a verb she uses takes a new argument, and tries the argument on her next write.\n\n## What to do\n\nRead the message when you read your box, then mark it processed once you have acted on it. If a note changes how you work, update your process: write the change as a `capture` on your own bot with `fields` `{\"starred\": \"true\"}`, so that your next boot carries it.\n\nNotes: list-read-pages-and-counts-ticks, many-entries-or-ticks-in-one-call, rhythm-on-the-calendar, trip-is-a-kind, defrag-ships-a-charter, release-notes','What changed: 6 notes','jojobot','2026-10-22T03:34:52.289603532Z','new',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL), ('kfps1g','assistant',5,'will be processed',NULL,'ad1vyx','2026-10-22T03:35:16.816111148Z','processed',NULL,NULL,3,'y2g86d',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL), ('tx95v4','assistant',6,'set aside as unreadable',NULL,'ad1vyx','2026-10-22T03:35:20.107860796Z','quarantined',NULL,NULL,2,'y2g86d','assistant','2026-10-22T03:35:20.152935163Z','recorded as unreadable',NULL);
